<!--
  DERIVED from bluemind-propfind-home-depth1-with-owner.xml (itself derived
  from the live capture bluemind-propfind-home-depth1.xml, 2026-08-20) and
  from the observation record of EXO-90234 (rig, BlueMind test server
  webmail.demo3.livecollab.fr, 2026-09-13 around 16:00, read-only PROPFINDs
  through eXo's relay; task 90234 comment 336361, note 52697 "Live results
  on BlueMind").

  What was observed and is reproduced here: after eXo user root (BlueMind
  FRANCOIS) had calendar CAL2 pushed as exo-cal-959b5529-ea4c-4ae4-a793-
  a2c201c3af9f, shared it read-only with Eric Meyer in the BlueMind UI, and
  Meyer subscribed, the Depth:1 listing of MEYER'S OWN home returned CAL2
  UNDER HIS HOME WITH THE SAME SLUG, with DAV:owner naming MEYER and the
  full owner privilege set (write, write-content, write-acl, ...). The
  subscribed resource "Véhicule de pool 1" (calendar:7E3AE6F3-...) was
  reported the same way. So on BlueMind neither the owner nor the privilege
  set tells a subscribed share from the user's own calendar.

  What is derived rather than captured: the home, the principal uid, the
  own calendars and the property shapes are the with-owner fixture's (the
  uid 9F3C1A20-... stands for the sharee's account, as it does for the
  account in that fixture); CAL2 and the pool vehicle are added as two
  responses in the same shape, with the slug, the display names and the
  resource uid prefix the observation recorded. The raw capture of Meyer's
  home is still owed. Read against CalendarCollection.isSharedWith(principal)
  and CaldavOutboundService.anchorOf(href): the server's two signals must
  stay silent on CAL2, and the anchor must be the one thing that names it.
-->
